  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 112 PM CDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nMuch of next week looks to remain fairly quiet with northwesterly \nflow aloft, but high 500 hPa heights, as we reside in-between a \nbroad expanse of high pressure out west and troughing over eastern \nNOAM. Given the prevailing northwest flow, moisture via the \nGulf or eastern Pacific will be limited, thus we're expecting \nmainly dry conditions into at least Thursday. This is great news\nfor our farmers who are wanting fields to dry to resume \nharvest. Plenty of solar insolation and at times gusty winds \nwill be favorable to accelerate soil drying. Temperatures will \nbe influenced by the passage of a couple of weak fronts, but \noverall look to be near to above normal with highs in the \n60s/70s and lows in the 40s/50s. Given 850 hPa temperature \nanomalies at or above 75th percentile and generally ranging from\n+2C to +8C, I wouldn't be surprised to see a couple of days \n(Tuesday/Wednesday) being warmer with a westerly and gusty \ncomponent to the winds owing to favorable downsloping off the \nHigh Plains.\n\nLate in the week there is the potential for rain chances to \nreturn, as the flow aloft turns more zonal allowing for an\ninflux of shortwave energy and eastern Pacific moisture. \nConfidence on timing and rain chances is low, however the good \nnews is should any rain occur it looks to be light with CMC/GFS \nensembles probabilities at 20% or less for >0.10\".",
